{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_017m49WZA5N4gjHr8bB8kJBG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the structures. Let me look at the `Tuple` struct and `LocalConnectionId` to understand the `p_local_cnxid->cnx_id` translation:\n\nIn C:\n```c\npicoquic_connection_id_t picoquic_get_local_cnxid(picoquic_cnx_t* cnx)\n{\n    return cnx->path[0]->first_tuple->p_local_cnxid->cnx_id;\n}\n```\n\nIn Rust:\n- `cnx->path[0]` → `connection.paths[0]`\n\n\n- `->first_tuple` → `.tuples[0]` since the linked list became a vector\n- `->p_local_cnxid->cnx_id` → `Tuple::local_connection_id` is an `Option<LocalConnectionIdToken>` that indexes into the `Connection::local_connection_ids` arena\n\nFor `picoquic_get_cnx_state`, I'm mapping `cnx->cnx_state` to `connection.connection_state`.\n\nFor `picoquic_get_next_cnx`, the C code just returns `cnx->next_in_table`, but in Rust the intrusive linked list is gone and connections live in an arena instead. Since there's no `next_in_table` field anymore, I need to iterate through the arena and return the next connection in order rather than doing a simple field lookup.\n\nFor the callback functions, I'm mapping `quic->default_callback_fn` and `quic->default_callback_ctx` to their Rust equivalents on the quic struct.  But these are boxed trait objects, not raw function pointers like the C API expects, so I need to figure out what shape these public API functions should actually take by looking at the conventions used elsewhere in lib.rs.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2934,"cache_read_input_tokens":50617,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2934},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc65f024-1847-48ea-8039-b3ad32