{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01FpAans3ngfhLriSDheg6Y6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the full picture. The C API provides two functions for traversal:\n1. `picoquic_get_first_cnx(quic)` - gets the first connection\n2. `picoquic_get_next_cnx(cnx)` - gets the next connection from a given connection\n\nIn the Rust translation, the `next_in_table` intrusive linked list is replaced by an Arena. The intrusive list traversal pattern can't be directly replicated without the back-pointer.\n\nThe key insight is that `Connection` doesn't have a `quic` back-pointer (the C `cnx->quic` pointer). \n\nSince the Arena-based approach means connections don't maintain a reference back to their Quic context, the Rust API fundamentally can't replicate the C semantics of `get_next_cnx` where you pass a single connection and get the next one. I need to look at how the arena structure actually stores these connections to figure out what's possible.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":644,"cache_read_input_tokens":63256,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":644},"output_tokens":37,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc65f024-1847-48ea-8039-b3ad326afc33","uuid":"b4b3760e-1407-4221-aef9-b65d5dd212f3"}
